variant was actually prevalent  and they did do some clinical trials in  south africa to assess  efficacy and we can see that against the  original strain  it's 72 which is much lower actually  than than the modern and pfizer but even  lower at 57 percent in the presence of  the of the south african strain of the  virus so so this is concerning obviously  for someone who who might receive this  vaccine same thing for the astrazeneca  vaccine where you can see that the  overall efficacy is 70 percent  against the original strain  but in south africa  there was only a 10 efficacy and they  actually stopped using the astrozenic  vaccine as a consequence of this it was  if it was as if no one was actually  getting any protection from vaccination  if we go  down the list further we can see that  the cinevac vaccine only has 50 efficacy  to the original strain  so if we look at all these data combined  again it begs the question if i receive  a certain vaccine  am i really protected post-vaccination  more real-world efficacy data which is a  little bit concerning is the elderly and  immunocompromised individuals and these  two studies here are  one was a study in germany performed on  people that were under 60 and over 80  years old and what we see here is if we  look at the negative cut off from the  from the serology test that they did  what they found was that many of the of  the people that were over 80 years old  were falling in this  not generating any significant  uh antibodies post  full vaccination  and this  this study used the pfizer vaccine and  these were all people who were fully  vaccinated uh where they where they  determined that that about 30 percent of  people over the the  over 80 years of age  don't generate measurable levels  of neutralizing antibodies in  another study which was performed in  canada and toronto  this was looking at immunocompromised  patients who are on immunosuppressive  drugs  after solid organ transplant and what  they found in this study was that over  70 percent of immunocompromised patients  receiving the modern vaccine did not  elicit a neutralizing antibody response  again this is very concerning it's not  very surprising because they were on  immunosuppressive  therapy so the immune system is already  compromised when they receive the  vaccine but still very concerning that  such a large proportion of people who  are on immunosuppressive therapy  are not having  a robust neutralizing antibody response  after vaccination  three major studies correlate vaccine  efficacy and protection so these are  very new studies that looked at vaccine  efficacy  versus  the neutralizing antibody levels in  those  individuals and  what these studies all find is that  there is a  kind of a cut off titer  somewhere around 100 to 200  wh international unit the  tighter the vaccine efficacy seems to  drop quite precipitously and and all of  these studies that have been done kind  of correlate uh each other uh pretty  well actually considering the large  variability uh in in in these studies  the corey paper also published in nature  medicine uh found that at a at 54  international units  protection is down to about 50  so again  quite well correlating with  each other all of these studies  so now if we look at  the comparison between binding assays  remember binding assays look at all the  antibodies that have been generated  by an immune response to a particular  antigen like receptor binding domain or  nucleocapsid protein or full spike  protein and if we compare those assays  to the neutralizing antibody test uh the  c-pass test that we've that we've  released  what we thought what we found was that  for two of these tests which are just  looking at igg binding  that  after using the pfizer vaccine  after two weeks  post uh the first injection of the  pfizer vaccine it looks like all the  patients almost 100 of them have a  strong antibody response but if you  compare those two immunoglobulin  binding tests to our neutralization test  which is this bought this bar in dark  blue we see we see a significant  difference much  less  people in the study group have  neutralizing antibodies in comparison to  the people that have binding antibodies  and with the moderna vaccine looking at  a more elderly population of course the  results are lower as expected because  elderly people tend to have a less  robust immune response to begin with  but  that said if you compare the  immunoglobulin binding antibody test to  the neutralization test you see a marked  decrease in  the number of people that have  neutralizing antibodies versus binding  antibodies  so it begs the question  should we be  more careful about the testing we do and  measure neutralizing antibodies to  assure which individuals have protective  neutralizing antibodies as opposed to  knowing that people just have binding  antibodies this article  where they looked at four individuals  and found that before the first  sorry after the first dose of vaccine  which is here  leading up to the second dose there was  a very low level of neutralizing  antibody titers  but  right at the second dose which is in  this gray zone here when all these  people got their second dose all these  lines go up as you see in tighter so so  very robust response  in in immunity after the second dose  which is good so lots of neutralizing  antibodies but then we measured uh their  response after the second dose and saw  that the titers dropped in some cases  quite precipitously so for patient 156  in other cases a little more gradually  so  it really does depend on  [Music]  response  how these titers drop and we still don't  know what level of titer can be a good  predictor of when to get a booster shot  but for sure the immune response  in terms of neutralizing antibody levels  is decreasing um  significantly after the second dose as  we see here  if we look at the case for israel which  which was one of the first countries to  actually receive a vaccination from the  from the pfizer vaccine  we know in in their case that  they  entered a fourth wave  of uh cases in august and that wave was  was as big as any of their other waves  and this was after their population had  been almost fully vaccinated with the  pfizer vaccine  so  there's definitely an issue of waning  immunity over time and particularly in  the presence of the variant so so most  of the  of their cases were from the delta  variant in this case  and they they initiated a booster  program and now their case load is  dropping down again almost to zero the  uk is now very much still in their  fourth wave  uh with again highly vaccinated  population pretty early on  and and again this suggests that the  immunity from that astrazeneca vaccine  is waning to the point where  people are now more susceptible  to the variant strains that are  circulating in the uk and the question  is is there a way now  to be more  um  more precise about measuring this like  when  people would need a booster shot as  opposed to as opposed to just  determining a random time frame for  people to be  to be boosted  so in conclusion  pcr testing only represents a moment in  time  when we get tested with a pcr test we  only know that we're negative at the  moment that we took the test  it doesn't mean that we are assured of  protection at any time after being  tested we could easily be infected at  any moment after a pcr test  the presence of binding antibodies also  does not mean that functional  neutralizing antibodies are present  we've we've learned this from the  previous study  here where we see that  you can have a lot of binding antibodies  but no neutralizing antibodies  furthermore a neutralizing  antibody can persist for months and  possibly longer as we saw from here  these are days after infection so this  after several months neutralizing  antibodies are still circulating in  these in in these patients  so a positive neutralizing  antibody test can indicate a persistent  level of frontline functional antibodies  that can potentially block the virus  from propagating and docking and  entering cells and propagating infection  so given that vaccine efficacy varies  widely a c-pass test may help to assure  that the expected  response to vaccination was elicited  and neutralizing antibodies can also be  measured longitudinally as we saw here  we can measure them over time to to  assess  how the antibody levels are waning and  whether protection  is still assured  so therefore for mixed vaccinated and  unvaccinated populations a combined  approach may be the best way to go about  testing now in this  post-vaccination  era  where we use pcr for those who don't  have immunity or have not been  vaccinated  and we can use c-pass or neutralizing  antibody tests for vaccinated  individuals or people who may have been  previously infected  to assess their levels of neutralizing  antibodies for more information we have  recently published a very short review  article in the journal of clinical  virology plus it's only a two and a half  page read it's it's written in very uh  plain language uh very well referenced  with the latest references and i  strongly recommend this article  as to put on your reading list  if you'd like to learn more about  anything related to this talk  and i appreciate your time and attention
 

